Z||000000||BPacholka||09-25-2001||03:56 PM||||Hi all, fresh meat here.<BR>I just bought my 1st Fiero and now wondering how I managed to live 40 years without one. My question is, is there any way to recalibrate the speedometer if I put larger tires on my car or do I just have to do the math and try to remember? In past cars I have had, I could get a replacement gear for the tranny. Thanks for the help,<BR>85 GT V6||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||09-25-2001||06:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BPacholka:<BR><B>Hi all, fresh meat here.<BR>I just bought my 1st Fiero and now wondering how I managed to live 40 years without one. My question is, is there any way to recalibrate the speedometer if I put larger tires on my car or do I just have to do the math and try to remember? In past cars I have had, I could get a replacement gear for the tranny. Thanks for the help,<BR>85 GT V6</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey another fellow Michigander!<BR>How big a change you going for?<BR>If you are going 13 inch to 14 or 15 inch, it isn't really that big a deal...<BR>But if you are going 16 or 17 inch, well then you may see it...<BR>After all, most speedos are not too accurate anyways...<BR>You can change gears on the Fiero too, just difficult to do...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||09-25-2001||07:09 PM||||It's not so much if it's a 14,15,16 or 17 inch wheel but the circumfrence of the tire that affects the speedo. Find a tire the same cir. as the stock ones and you'll not have to change a thing. If change you must, there is a company that makes an inline doohicky to adjust the pulses/mile (Dakota something I think)||||reg||1
Z||000003||jch82||09-25-2001||07:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BPacholka:<BR><B>Hi all, fresh meat here.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Woohoo! We love fresh meat!!  jk  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Welcome to the forum. Good to see another Michigander!!<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>85 GT V6</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Got any pics?? I have an 85GT as well....Love mine to death!!<P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jch82.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000004||terryk||09-25-2001||10:46 PM||||Dakota Digital. There are others.||||reg||1
Z||000005||JSocha||09-26-2001||03:07 PM||||Somebody mentioned "Dakota!".  I feel special!<P>Welcome to the forum BPacholka. Michigan's "Fresh Meat"?!?!  Didn't think there was any fresh meat left in Michigan.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Oh well, I'm with Emirl, "Pork Fat Rules!"<P>I've been wondering about calibration to since I went through Nebraska and got my speed read by their setup stations.  Mechanics here say there is nothing they can do on the Fiero speedo, including a GM mechanic.  <P>My 86GT is roughly 4 to 5 miles off.  I have to go 74/75 on the speedomter to be doing 70MPH.  <P>P205/60R15 on all the 16" rims of the 86 which I now swaped with my 87 because the rims were better on the 86 (step daughter college car who needed better tires versus rims) and my tires were better on my 87.<P>My 87 (16" rims) had P215/60R15 and P205/60R15 which are now on the 86.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 09-26-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000006||MinnGreenGT||09-26-2001||03:49 PM||||Use this calculator...It'll tell you which size rim/tire combo will give you the most accuracy on the speedo.  (I know is says for the "miata" on the webpage, but the wheel/tire formula works on every vehicle!)<BR> <A HREF="http://www.miata.net/garage/tirecalc.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.miata.net/garage/tirecalc.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fullstaff.gif">   <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
